NAME
       assert - stop the program when a condition does not hold

SYNOPSIS
       #include <assert.h>

       void assert(scalar expression);

DESCRIPTION
       If the expression is false, assert prints the text of the
       expression, the file, the line and the function to stderr and
       aborts. If it is true, nothing happens.

       Compiling with -DNDEBUG turns every assert into nothing, so the
       expression is not evaluated at all. Never put work inside an
       assert that the program depends on:

         assert(read(fd, buf, n) == n);

       reads nothing once NDEBUG is defined.

RETURN VALUE
       None. An assert that fails does not return.

SEE ALSO
       abort(3), err(3)
